{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.72,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 13, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"}
      ],
      "why": "The only family with proven feasibility and the sole contributor to the merged front (54/73 feasible, best area 45673 um^2, 14/14 front points). It is also the only 1-result/cycle family whose Fmax comfortably clears the batch window: with 1 result/cycle the 32-request tick needs (n_iter+33)/fmax_us <= 0.5, i.e. fmax >= 2*(n_iter+33) MHz (>= ~102 MHz at n_iter=18), which a fully-pipelined micro-rotation chain reaches even at the wide end, and throughput >= 32 Msps is automatic. Spreading data_width 13-20 spans the 2^-12 error floor (LSB 2^-(W-2), so W>=14 is needed and W>=15 gives rounding margin) while staying out of the large-W/high-N corner that only buys area; n_iter 8-22 brackets the convergence-limited accuracy knee so the low-area end of the front is mapped; angle_guard and frac_guard are kept near zero because they cost datapath width without adding recoverable accuracy. This is where the extra evals should go: refine the low-area edge of the accuracy-vs-area trade-off, which is what 'select: min area' consumes, while preserving HV coverage of the accuracy tail."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.28,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 13, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 2, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 2, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 5, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
      ],
      "why": "It shares pipelined's bit-exact accuracy and its 1-result/cycle rate, so it is the only other family that can satisfy the 32-in-0.5 us burst at all, and registering every m stages replaces ~n_iter register stages with ~n_iter/m, which is exactly the lever for 'min area' if Fmax survives. It is 0/27 feasible so far, so the share is deliberately modest and the ranges are pushed to survivability: small m (2-5, with m>=2 as the only regime that keeps the critical path short enough to still meet fmax >= 2*(ceil(n_iter/m)+33) MHz for the burst) and small guard bits (the m>1 chain already lengthens the combinational path, so extra angle/frac width is pure cost). If round 1 confirms no feasible pipelined_m point, its remaining share should be folded into pipelined rather than spending it on the m>=6 regime, which can only lower Fmax further."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Two families only, both the 1-result/cycle kind, because the binding system constraint is the 1 MHz tick releasing 32 requests that must all land within 0.5 us: a design that emits one result every k cycles would need roughly 32*k cycles for the burst. Pipelined/pipelined_m emit one per cycle, so the burst reduces to (n_iter(+ceil/m)+2+31)/fmax <= 0.5 us plus a trivial 32 Msps throughput floor (fmax >= 32 MHz, comfortably below any realistic pipelined Fmax). Accuracy is family-independent (identical bits for the same data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding) and the spec floor max_abs_err <= 2^-12 means data_width >= 14 with round; the search therefore only has to trade n_iter/width/guards against area. pipelined already owns the entire merged front (14/14, best 45673 um^2) and has 74% feasibility, so it gets ~72% of the budget and a broad-but-focused box whose upper n_iter/width edges are clipped where accuracy saturates and area only grows; ~28% goes to pipelined_m, the only plausible route to a smaller FF count, restricted to small m and small guards so that Fmax can still clear the burst window, with the intent to reallocate that share to pipelined next round if it again yields zero feasible points. Round-1 plan: 100 evals split pipelined 72 / pipelined_m 28, leaving ~72/28 for rounds 2-3 to refine the front before the reserved full-range mapping pass."
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "rationale": "Round 1 leaves 100 of 200 evals. The feasible front is entirely pipelined (8 designs) and covers only a slice of both objective ranges: area_um2 40877..61802 against the HV reference 100000, and accuracy_bits 12.3..16.1 while the registry permits far higher accuracy (data_width on the front is only 17..20 of 8..28). The min-area corner (40877 um^2, 12.3 bits) already sits just above the 2^-12 error constraint, so there is little room to shrink area, but the curve is truncated on the high-accuracy side, which is exactly where hypervolume is being left on the table. pipelined_m produced 0 feasible designs in 28 evals (best accuracy 13.02 bits, max throughput 91.1 MSPS) so it is not obviously a source of new front points, and no family remains unexplored. The best use of the remaining budget is therefore to let NSGA-II map the full front over the full ranges of the pipelined family (seeded with the current front), extending accuracy_bits well past 16 and filling the area range up to the reference point, rather than narrowing around the already-dense low-area region.",
  "next_plan": null
}
